\wg21_head(
  title = {Revisiting attribute placement on Contracts}
){
\dl{
  \dt{Document number:} \dd{\docnum{P4456R0}}
  \dt{Date:}            \dd{\tt{2026-11-05}}
  \dt{Audience:}        \dd{EWG}
  \dt{Project:}         \dd{ISO/IEC 14882 Programming Languages — C++, ISO/IEC JTC1/SC22/WG21}
  \dt{Reply-to:}        \dd{Jan Schultke <\mail{janschultke@gmail.com}>}
  \dt{GitHub Issue:}    \dd{\ref("https://wg21.link/P4456/github")}
  \dt{Source:}          \dd{\ref("https://github.com/eisenwave/cpp-proposals/blob/main/src/contracts-attributes.cow")}
}
\hr
}

\Babstract{
This paper explores potential resolutions to NB comment FR02-077 on the C++26 DIS.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

FR02-077 states:
\Bquote{
The syntax is still not in phase with the compromise
that was found in the liaison study group between WG21 and WG14:
attributes are in the wrong place and hurt possible processing in a future C version of contracts.

\b{Suggested change:}

Adopt the syntax for attribute discussed in the liaison study group.
}

The change would look as follows:

\cppblock{
void f() pre \del{[[attrib]]} (true);  // N5054
void f() pre(true) \ins{[[attrib]]};   // FR02-077
}

That is, the position of the \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq}
would move from after \tcode{pre} or \tcode{post}
to the end of the \grammarterm{precondition-specifier} or \grammarterm{postcondition-specifier}.

While this change was not discussed or polled during an SG22 telecon,
it can be considered a \q{compromise} because it increases compatibility
with the in-development C version of contracts in \ref("WG14-N3929"),
with the following C syntax:

\cowel_macro("_Pre"){\cowel_highlight_as("keyword"){_Pre}}

\cppblock{
void f(int n) \_Pre()(n > 0);
// or
#include <stdc_contract.h> // provides #define pre(...) _Pre(__VA_ARGS__)
void f(int n) pre(n > 0);
}

Notably, the current C++ syntax is incompatible with the \tcode{#define pre(...)} approach
because an attribute between \tcode{pre} and the parameter list
would prevent macro substitution.
Earlier revision of \ref("WG14-N3929") actually supported an attribute list
in the same location as C++,
i.e. \tcode{\_Pre [[attrib]] (true)} would be valid,
but this was removed in later revisions.
The current paper doesn't support attributes in any position,
but the suggested suffix syntax may result in them being reintroduced.

\Bnote{
If C had contracts and
if \header{stdc_contract.h} were included in the C++ standard,
this would presumably be done as a header with no effect,
similar to \header{stdbool.h} in the past,
which defined \tcode{true} in C while having no effect in C++.

Making the C++ header also provide \tcode{#define post(...)}
would be highly problematic because it would affect many existing functions
such as \tcode{boost::asio::post}.
In C, it is common practice to prefix the identifiers themselves with a library-specific prefix,
whereas C++ developers generally assume that these short identifiers are safe to use
(at least as a member function or in a namespace).
}

\h2{Possible solutions}

At this point, there are three plausible solutions to this situation:
\ol{
  \li{
    Keep attributes where they are, as proposed in \ref("P2900R14").
  }
  \li{
    Move attributes to the location suggested in FR02-077,
    i.e. to the end of the \grammarterm{precondition-specifier}.
  }
  \li{
    Remove attributes from contracts entirely for the time being and revisit the issue in C++29.
  }
}

Find below an argument for each of the possible solutions.

\h3{Keep attributes where they are}

The placement of attributes on Contracts
was explored extensively in \ref("P3088R1").

\h4{Readability of current syntax}

On the subject of the suffix location, \ref("P3088R1") states the following:

\Bquote{
We could consider placing the \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq} after the contract assertion:
\cppblock{
bool binary_search(Range r, const T& value)
  pre (is_sorted(r)) [[vendor::audit]];
void f() {
  int i = get_i();
  contract_assert (i > 0) [[vendor::assume]];
  // ...
}
}
While the suffix location seems technically possible — as far as we can tell,
it does not create any outright collisions with existing grammar — it is far from ideal,
in particularly because, just like the prefix location,
it is not obvious to the reader what the attribute appertains to:
\cppblock{
// which pre does the attribute appertain to?
void f(int* i)
  pre(i != nullptr) [[vendor::xxx]] pre (*i > 0);
  // does the attribute appertain to the pre or to the function g as a whole?
void g(int j) pre(j > 0) [[vendor::yyy]];
}
We therefore do not propose the suffix location.
}

\h4{C compatibility is limited anyway}

Two extensions for Contracts are already in the pipeline for C++29
which would also make the \tcode{#define} approach incompatible:
\ul{
  \li{
    \ref("P3098R2") \q{Contracts for C++: Postcondition captures} adds:
    \Bindent{\itemdecl{post [old_size = size()] (size() == old_size + 1)}}
    The new \grammarterm{contract-capture-clause} following \tcode{post}
    inhibits macro expansion of \tcode{#define post(...)}.
  }
  \li{
    \ref("P3400R5") \q{Controlling Contract-Assertion Properties} adds:
    \Bindent{\itemdecl{pre<audit>(std::is_sorted(begin, end))}}
    The new \grammarterm{assertion-control-specifier} inhibits macro expansion.
  }
}

WG21 has never agreed to have an approach that is entirely compatible
with the macro-based solution in C.
Doing so would require no future extension to go between
\tcode{pre} and the following parentheses,
but this location is an obvious choice
considering the precedent set by e.g. \tcode{static_cast<...>()}.

Furthermore, C compatibility with the current C++26 syntax is not out of the question.
There at least two possible approaches:
\ul{
  \li{
    Make \tcode{pre} a contextual keyword in C, just like in C++,
    rather than pursuing a \tcode{#define pre(...)} approach.
  }
  \li{
    Instead provide \tcode{#define pre _Pre}.
    However, this would affect any use of the identifier,
    including the use of \tcode{pre} as a variable name.
  }
}

One should also understand that the \ref("WG14-N3929") approach is only
superficially and syntactically compatible with C++ Contracts:
there is no support for ignore semantic,
there is an additional \tcode{\cowel_highlight_as("keyword"){_Inv}} specifier
that combines preconditions and postconditions, etc.
The author of \ref("WG14-N3929") does not seek to maximize C++ compatibility
but vehemently opposes design principles of \ref("P2900R14") such as ignorability.
Even if attributes were moved to the proposed location,
C++ Contracts with ignore semantic would remain incompatible.

\Bnote{
Ignored preconditions can still be emulated in C just like for \tcode{assert}:
\cppblock{
#ifdef __cplusplus
  // do nothing in C++
#elifdef NDEBUG
  #define pre(...) _Pre(true)        // ignore on release
#else
  #define pre(...) _Pre(__VA_ARGS__) // enforce on debug
#endif

void f() pre(cond) [[attrib]];
}
Assuming that the suffix syntax for attributes was used in both languages
and that (quick-)enforce semantic is used in C++
this approach is compatible.
It is important that \tcode{pre} does not expand to an empty token sequence
because the following \tcode{[[attrib]]} would otherwise apply to the function.

This approach is well-formed even with \header{stdc_contract.h} included,
but probably not intended and may break in the future:
An obviously correct alternative is:
\cppblock{
#ifdef __cplusplus
  #define debug_pre(...) pre<debug>(__VA_ARGS__) // P3400
#elifdef NDEBUG
  #define debug_pre(...) _Pre(true)        // ignore on release
#else
  #define debug_pre(...) _Pre(__VA_ARGS__) // enforce on debug
#endif
}
}

\h4{Breaking the GCC extension}

As an extension, GCC \em{already} allows the placement of attributes
after the \grammarterm{function-contract-specifier-seq}
on non-defining declarations,
meaning that the following code is valid:
\cppblock{
void f() pre [[standard]] (true) [[wtf]]; // [[wtf]] appertains to f()
}

Making the suffix syntax valid now would
\ul{
  \li{silently change what \tcode{[[wtf]]} appertains to, and}
  \li{break existing code which places attributes like \tcode{[[standard]]}.}
}

However, these breaking changes only affect C++26 code,
which should be considered experimental.

\h4{Macro hazards}

It is plausible that a user would write code along the following lines
if the suffix syntax for attributes was available:

\cppblock{
#ifdef FEATURE_ACTIVATED
  #define PRE(...) pre(__VA_ARGS__)
#else
  #define PRE(...) // nothing
#endif

void f(int i) \cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){PRE}(i >= 0) [[attrib]];
}

The obvious issue is that if \tcode{\cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){PRE}} expands to nothing,
the following \tcode{[[attrib]]} applies to \tcode{f()}.
\em{Someone} will get this wrong eventually
(\ref("https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murphy%27s_law"){Murphy's law}).
The existing syntax prevents that scenario because
\tcode{\cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){PRE}} would have to produce \tcode{[[attrib]]}, or
the entire \grammarterm{function-contract-specifier-seq} would need to be wrapped
in an \tcode{#ifdef} block.

\h4{Fool-proof syntax}

The attribute placement in C++ is anything but obvious,
sometimes resulting in people randomly guessing or misremembering where attributes go.
A major issue with the suffix syntax is that it would accept the \q{random guess}
of putting an attribute at the very end:
\cppblock{
void f() pre(true) [[attrib]];
}

This may be problematic when an attribute has different meaning
depending on whether it appertains to a precondition or to the function type,
and it's quite plausible that \tcode{[[attrib]]} was intended to apply to \tcode{f()} instead.

By comparison, the syntax \tcode{pre [[attrib]] (true)} is more fool-proof
because the attribute is \q{sandwiched} inside of the \grammarterm{precondition-specifier},
so it obviously applies to the precondition.
It is also hard to imagine someone placing an attribute immediately following \tcode{pre}
as the result of a random guess.

\h4{Perfect forward compatibility}

Another argument in support of the current syntax
is the perfect forward compatibility.
That is, because \tcode{[[attrib]]}
is placed immediately after the \tcode{pre} keyword,
we can ensure that no matter where contracts are placed in the future,
an attribute would never cause ambiguity.
By contrast, prefix and suffix notation either cause ambiguities already
or could theoretically cause ambiguities in the future:
\cppblock{
// With prefix syntax, this would be ambiguous:
void f() [[attrib]] pre(true);

// With suffix syntax, a hypothetical future construct
// with prefix attributes could be ambiguous:
void f() pre(true) [[attrib]];                // OK
[[attrib]] \i{\cowel_highlight_as("keyword"){hypothetical-construct}};            // OK
pre(true) [[attrib]] \i{\cowel_highlight_as("keyword"){hypothetical-construct}};  // ambiguous
}

That being said, there are no concrete suggestions for a
\tcode{\i{\cowel_highlight_as("keyword"){hypothetical-construct}}}
affected by suffix syntax … yet.

\h3{Suffix syntax}

\h4{C compatibility of suffix syntax}

The immediate benefit of the suffix syntax is that it maintains compatibility
with the \ref("WG14-N3929") \q{macro approach} of \tcode{#define pre(...)}.
While future extensions like \ref("P3098R2") and \ref("P3400R5")
may introduce further syntax that makes the macro approach incompatible,
the current attribute placement is the only syntactical incompatibility
that already exists today in C++26.
Relocating the attribute as FR02-077 suggests would thus give WG14 and WG21
until C++29 to find a solution.

That being said,
we must not make hasty technical decisions solely motivated by deadlines,
and given the planned future extensions,
the current macro approach seems unsustainable anyway.

\h4{Readability of suffix syntax}

Even purely looking at C++,
the suffix syntax can be justified with technical arguments.
The above example that \ref("P3088R1") provides to demonstrate confusion can also be formatted as:

\cppblock{
void f(int* i)
  pre(i != nullptr) [[vendor::something]]
  pre(*i > 0) [[vendor::something]];
}

\Bnote{
This example results in undefined behavior with observe semantic,
but that's not relevant to the syntactical discussion.
}

With this style,
it is obvious what the attributes appertain to.
The rule that \tcode{pre} and \tcode{post} are at the beginning of a new line
can also be enforced by auto-formatters.
With vendor attributes in place,
preconditions may take up so much space that such multi-line formatting is desirable
and often necessary to fit within a column limit anyway.

\h4{Suffix syntax combined with captures and labels}

We must consider how a suffix syntax composes with the future extensions
in \ref("P3098R2") and \ref("P3400R5"):
\cppblock{
void f(int i)
  pre<audit>(i < size())                      [[vendor::something]]  // P3400
  post[old_size = size()](size() == old_size) [[vendor::something]]; // P3098
}

For reference,
the syntax currently proposed in those papers places attributes as follows:
\cppblock{
void f(int i)
  pre<audit> [[vendor::something]] (i < size())                       // P3400
  post [[vendor::something]] [old_size = size()](size() == old_size); // P3098
}

There are several reasons to prefer to the suffix syntax:

\ol{
  \li{
    The information provided by attributes is pushed to the edge of the screen.
    This makes sense because information provided by attributes
    is typically less important than any of the other elements of a contract.
    Attributes can often be ignored by some readers,
    so placing them immediately after \tcode{pre} is annoying.
    \Bex{
    Concretely, attributes could provide custom diagnostic strings in the future.
    The status quo would look as follows:
\cppblock{
void f()
  pre [[message("i must be positive")]] (i >= 0);
}
This appears undesirable;
the failure message is by far the least important bit of information,
but it receives a dominant position.
This improves with a suffix syntax:
\cppblock{
void f()
  pre(i >= 0) [[message("i must be positive")]];
}
    Note that this specific example would become irrelevant
    if \ref("P3099R3") \q{Contracts for C++: User-defined Diagnostic Messages} were accepted,
    which provides the syntax:
\cppblock{
void f()
  pre(i >= 0, "i must be positive");
}
}
  }
  \li{
    The suffix syntax vaguely follows precedent established by functions,
    at least if no \tcode{const} or \tcode{noexcept} is applied.
    That is, a \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq} appears
    at the end of \grammarterm{parameters-and-qualifiers}.
    It is unclear what precedent \ref("P3400R5") relies on,
    as it has the syntax \tcode{pre<audit>[[attrib]](true)}.
    This is surprising because there is
    \q{the attribute goes after the keyword}
    precedent set by \tcode{struct [[attrib]] S {}}
    (i.e. in a \grammarterm{class-head}).
    This precedent is followed by \ref("P3098R2") but not by \ref("P3400R5").
  }
  \li{
    With the current syntax,
    it is not obvious to users whether \tcode{[[attrib]]}
    should be placed before or after the captures in a postcondition.
    Note that lambdas have the syntax \tcode{[x=0] [[attrib]] {}},
    i.e. attributes go after the captures.
    However, \ref("P3098R2") proposes the opposite ordering.
    Both relative orderings of captures and attributes
    are counter-intuitive in their own way.
    Even if attributes were placed after the postcondition captures
    (hypothetically),
    this could create large visual distance between the captures and
    the condition that references them,
    decreasing readability.
    For example, consider:
\cppblock{
void f() post[x=0] [[vendor::something, vendor::other_thing]] (x > 0);
}
    While this is \u{not} the current direction of \ref("P3098R2"),
    it is an option for C++29 as an extension of the current syntax,
    so worth mentioning here.
  }
  \li{
    Regardless of the relative ordering in the current syntax,
    it is problematic to have two square-brackets constructs
    immediately following one another because it leaves the author with two options:
    \ul{
      \li{
        Write \tcode{post[[attrib]][x=0](...)} compactly,
        which results in visual clutter.
      }
      \li{
        Write \tcode{post [[attrib]] [x=0](...)},
        i.e. create space around the attribute for readability.
        However, this takes up more space and is inconsistent
        with the otherwise compact \tcode{post(...)} notation that most people would use.
      }
    }
    The suffix notation isolates the attribute and
    avoids putting it next to other square brackets.
  }
}

\h4{Suffix syntax for macro-expanded pre- and postconditions}

The suffix syntax allows for \tcode{pre} with \ref("P3400R5") labels
to be expanded from macros,
with attributes placed outside:
\cppblock{
#define MY_LIB_DEBUG_PRE(...) pre<::my_lib::contracts::debug>(__VA_ARGS__)
#define MY_LIB_RELEASE_PRE(...) pre<::my_lib::contracts::release>(__VA_ARGS__)

void f(int i)
  // attribute appertains to pre<::my_lib::contracts::debug>
  \cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){MY_LIB_DEBUG_PRE}(i >= 0) [[attrib]];
}

By comparison, the current syntax makes it difficult to apply attributes
to macro-expanded pre- and postconditions.

\Bex{
A concrete example would be a postcondition that is guarded behind a feature-test macro
because it only becomes spellable once there are \tcode{post} captures:
\cppblock{
#if __cpp_contracts >= 202808L
  #define CAP_POST(cap, ...) post[cap](__VA_ARGS__) // yay!
#else
  #define CAP_POST(cap, ...) post(true)             // can't spell it yet :(
#endif

void f() \cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){CAP_POST}(old=size(), old <= size()) [[attrib]];
}
Note that this example would not work
if we expanded \tcode{\cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){CAP_POST}} to nothing.
}

\Bex{
Similarly, some users may not be comfortable with the current \tcode{pre}
but would use the always-enforced contracts once available.
After all, the lack of always-enforced contracts is one of the most common criticisms.
However, with macros, we could write preconditions that are always enforced
if such a semantic is available,
or at the very least, are not evaluated with observe semantic.
This leads to the following macro-expanded preconditions:
\cppblock{
#if __cpp_contracts >= 202808L
  // P3400.
  #define PRE(...) pre<enforce>(__VA_ARGS__)
#elifdef __builtin_contract_semantic_is_enforced
  // Using an extension that informs us about the contract evaluation semantic.
  #define PRE(...) pre(__VA_ARGS__)
#else
  // Otherwise, disable.
  // Alternatively, we could use #error here
  // if we wanted a hard "it's enforced or it errors" guarantee.
  #define PRE(...) pre(true)
#endif

void f(int i) \cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){PRE}(i >= 0) [[attrib]];
}
}

\Bex{
If \ref("P3099R3") were accepted,
users may write:
\cppblock{
#ifdef __cpp_contracts_message
  // High-tech solution: P3099.
  #define PRE_MSG(expr, msg) pre(expr, msg)
#else
  // Low-tech solution: rely on conversion of string literals to bool
  // to make the message part of the expression.
  #define PRE_MSG(expr, msg) pre(bool(expr) && bool(msg))
#endif

void f(int i) \cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){PRE_MSG}(i >= 0, "i must be positive") [[attrib]];
}
}

Since Contracts are not yet in their final state of completion,
there will undoubtedly be features
that force users to guard certain pre- and postconditions behind feature-test macros.
While making a new \tcode{#ifdef} block is always possible,
it is much more noisy than expanding only the postcondition itself
from a macro like \tcode{\cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){CAP_POST}}.

\h3{Remove attributes}

Another option is to remove attributes from contracts entirely.
This would give WG21 more time to discuss possible designs and
more time to coordinate with WG14.
In spirit,
removal would resolve FR02-077
because C++26 would introduce no incompatibility with the current C design.

While the option is drastic,
there are no standard attributes that motivate attribute usage on contracts.
The only reason \ref("P3088R1") supports attributes
is to allow for vendor-specific attributes,
similar to how \ref("CWG2262") added attributes for \tcode{asm} statements.
Despite \ref("P3088R1") providing extensive motivation
in the form of possible \tcode{[[vendor::audit]]}, \tcode{[[vendor::assume]]}, etc. attributes,
there may not be sufficient urgency to justify keeping attributes in C++26.

However, attributes have been added
specifically because vendors have requested a standard location,
not \q{just because},
so the motivation for removal would have to be really compelling.

\h3{Note on grammatical ambiguity}

To be clear,
both the current syntax and the FR02-077 suffix syntax have no grammatical ambiguity.

\Bex{
To provide attributes that appertain to a declaration,
a function type, and
a precondition, at the same time, one can write:
\cppblock{
[[dcl_attrib]] void f() [[f_attrib]] pre [[contract_attrib]] (true); // N5054
[[dcl_attrib]] void f() [[f_attrib]] pre(true) [[contract_attrib]];  // FR02-077
}
}

\Bex{
To provide an attribute that appertains to an \grammarterm{alias-declaration},
following \ref("P3583R0") \q{Contracts, Types & Functions}, one can write:
\cppblock{
using T [[alias_attrib]] = void f() [[contract_attrib]] pre(true); // N5054
using T [[alias_attrib]] = void f() pre(true) [[contract_attrib]]; // FR02-077
}
Currently, this is ill-formed either way because contracts cannot be applied
to function types or function pointers.
The key observation is that \tcode{[[alias_attrib]]} does not appear
at the end of the whole \grammarterm{alias-declaration},
so it wouldn't be ambiguous with the suffix syntax.
}

The only \em{true} ambiguity would be introduced by a prefix syntax
because in \tcode{void f() [[attrib]] pre(true)},
the attribute could appertain to either the function type or the precondition.

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").
Wording is provided for both the suffix syntax and for removal of attributes.

\h3{Wording for suffix syntax}

Change the grammar in \eelis{dcl.contract.func} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\dl(class="grammar"){
  \dt{\grammarterm{function-contract-specifier-seq}:}
  \dd{function-contract-specifier \opt{function-contract-specifier-seq}}

  \dt{\grammarterm{function-contract-specifier}:}
  \dd{precondition-specifier}
  \dd{postcondition-specifier}

  \dt{\grammarterm{precondition-specifier}:}
  \dd{
    \tcode{pre} \del{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}} \tcode{(} conditional-expression \tcode{)} \ins{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}}
  }

  \dt{\grammarterm{postcondition-specifier}:}
  \dd{
    \tcode{post} \del{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}} \tcode{(} \opt{result-name-introducer} conditional-expression \tcode{)} \ins{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}}
  }
}
}

Change the grammar in \eelis{stmt.contract.assert} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\dl(class="grammar"){
  \dt{\grammarterm{assertion-statement}:}
  \dd{
    \tcode{contract_assert} \del{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}} \tcode{(} conditional-expression \tcode{)} \ins{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}}
  }
}
}

Change \eelis{dcl.contract.func#3} as follows:

\Bdiff{
Each \grammarterm{function-contract-specifier}
of a \grammarterm{function-contract-specifier-seq} (if any)
of an unspecified first declaration\iref{basic.def}
of a function introduces a corresponding function contract assertion for that function.
The optional \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq}
\del{following \tcode{pre} or \tcode{post}}
\ins{of a \grammarterm{precondition-specifier} or \grammarterm{postcondition-specifier}}
appertains to the introduced contract assertion.
}

Bump the feature-test macro in \eelis{cpp.predefined} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\itemdecl{
__cpp_contracts \del{202606L} \ins{202611L}
}
}

\h3{Wording for removal}

Change the grammar in \eelis{dcl.contract.func} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\dl(class="grammar"){
  \dt{\grammarterm{function-contract-specifier-seq}:}
  \dd{function-contract-specifier \opt{function-contract-specifier-seq}}

  \dt{\grammarterm{function-contract-specifier}:}
  \dd{precondition-specifier}
  \dd{postcondition-specifier}

  \dt{\grammarterm{precondition-specifier}:}
  \dd{
    \tcode{pre} \del{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}} \tcode{(} conditional-expression \tcode{)}
  }

  \dt{\grammarterm{postcondition-specifier}:}
  \dd{
    \tcode{post} \del{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}} \tcode{(} \opt{result-name-introducer} conditional-expression \tcode{)}
  }
}
}

Change \eelis{dcl.contract.func#3} as follows:

\Bdiff{
Each \grammarterm{function-contract-specifier}
of a \grammarterm{function-contract-specifier-seq} (if any)
of an unspecified first declaration\iref{basic.def}
of a function introduces a corresponding function contract assertion for that function.
\del{The optional \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq}
following \tcode{pre} or \tcode{post}
appertains to the introduced contract assertion.}
}

Change \eelis{stmt.contract.assert} as follows:

\Bdiff{
\dl(class="grammar"){
  \dt{\grammarterm{assertion-statement}:}
  \dd{
    \tcode{contract_assert} \del{\opt{attribute-specifier-seq}} \tcode{(} conditional-expression \tcode{)}
  }
}
}

Change \eelis{stmt.contract.assert#1} as follows:

\Bdiff{
An \grammarterm{assertion-statement} introduces a contract assertion\iref{basic.contract}.
\del{The optional \grammarterm{attribute-specifier-seq}
appertains to the introduced contract assertion.}
}

\editnote{
The feature-test macro is not bumped.
Doing so would be pointless because there is no new capability after this proposal is applied.
Effectively, it is as if attributes never existed on contracts in the first place.
}

